  - Complex plane
    - Sound exists in the real world, so why are we talking about the complex plane, which is distinguished by the introduction of "imaginary" numbers?
    - The complex plane turns out to be a useful analytical tool for modeling periodic phenomena such as audio signals (even real-world ones)
    - The key connection (cite McFee DST Ch 4): rotation is the fundamental periodic phenomenon, and multiplication in the complex plane models rotation
    - Scope: Complex numbers are assumed background knowledge for this book, however, we revisit the basics here in case you're a bit rusty (which is fine).
    - Introduce j (not i) as imaginary constant
      - Tip: In this book, prefer to think of j as an "analytical tool" for better understanding real-valued signals, rather than an "imaginary number". This framing can help demystify the concept
    - Basic Cartesian form and operations (addition, multiplication), see and cite https://brianmcfee.net/dstbook-site/content/ch04-complex/Basic-Operations.html but be much more succinct
    - Basic conversion between Cartesian and Polar form
    - Multiplication in polar form (magnitudes multiply and angles add)
    - Euler's formula, form adopted from https://brianmcfee.net/dstbook-site/content/ch04-complex/Exponentials.html
  - Phasor
    - Now that we've reviewed complex plane, let's bring this back into a sound context
    - Recall the basic sinusoid from chapter 3: a cos(\omega t), the most elementary periodic sound. In chapter 1 we used a sin(\omega t + \phi) rather than a cos(\omega t), but here we'll use this other form for reasons that will momentarily become clear
      - Also, recall that cos(\omega t) = sin(\omega t + \pi/2), so we're basically just setting the initial phase here
      - Tip: recall that \omega is angular frequency in (unit) radians per second. if you forgot this, it's time to revisit {ref}`angular_frequency` (add this anchor in chapter 3), as we'll be regularly be using angular frequency going forward for brevity (avoids writing $2\pi f$ everywhere)
    - Suppose we wanted to transform this basic sinusoid so that it operates in the complex plane, how might we do that?
    - Let's apply Euler's formula!
      - e^{j\theta} = cos(\theta) + jsin(\theta)
      - a e^{j\theta} = ... (multiply both sides by a)
      - a e^{j \omega t} = ... (make \theta a function of time w/ angular frequency \omega, i.e., \theta(t) = \omega t)
    - What have we accomplished here? A few things!
      - (1) We brought our basic sinusoid into the complex plane, by complementing it w/ an _analytical tool_: j \cdot a \sin (\omega t). While our basic sinusoid is always real-valued, this tool is always imaginary-valued, and always exactly \pi/2 radians "out of phase" with our basic sinusoid
      - (2) We used Euler's formula to derive a _complex sinusoid_ (vocab), a compact expression capturing the two sinusoids as a single complex exponential
    - What does it look like?
      - Embed TODO in the text: TAs should create an interactive animation using pyodide. Two sliders: frequency f (Hertz) and a (amplitude). plot e^{j \cdot 2\pi f t}, project real/imag component to show sinusoids being traced, just like the GIF on the slides
      - Commonly referred to as a _phasor_
      - Plainly, it is a vector of radius a the rotates counter clockwise and draws the outline of a circle
      - It completes a rotation every 1/f seconds, where f = \omega / 2\pi
      - Study this, play around with the code example. Deriving the phasor is the main reason we're studying the complex plane here. Hopefully it's not too scary after all - it's literally just a fancy way to draw a circle repeatedly!
    - Define complex sinusoid or phasor as a \cdot e^{j \omega t}: \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{C}
      - Important: like the basic sinusoid, a phasor is a function of _time_, not of _frequency_. the only difference is that it rotates in the complex plane instead
    - **The _complex sinusoid_ is perhaps the most important expression in computer music**, capturing both the periodic ingredient of sound in the basic sinusoid, and as you will soon see, giving rise to the Fourier transform which allows us to uncover a unique sinusoidal recipe for any sound
  - Fourier Transform
    - Define X(\omega), the Fourier transform of x(t)
    - Finally, a function of _frequency_! Frequency is an input to the function, and the output is a single complex number \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{C}
    - Note: Calculus is not the focus of this book. The Fourier transform does include an integral, but you will not need to work through tricky integration here. We will instead eventually derive a version of the Fourier transform that acts as a tractable computational tool, not a mathematical one
    - Break it down into R(\omega) and I(\omega), and succinctly equate X(\omega) to these two expressions via Euler's formula
