  - Amplitude and phase spectrum
    - Our goal at the start of this chapter was to answer the question "how much of a given frequency is in some unknown sound?", i.e., uncover the sinusoidal recipe of a sound
    - The Fourier transform sort of gives us this, but the output is a complex number. How do we determine its amplitude?
    - Fairly straightforward, just convert Cartesian form to Polar form!
    - This gives us amplitude and phase spectrum |X(\omega)| and \ang X(\omega)
    - Recall from Chapter 3 (anchor ref) that our ear is much more sensitive to amplitude than it is to phase, so the amplitude spectrum is far more common
    - You may already be familiar with the amplitude spectrum if you've ever used a "spectrum analyzer" in a digital audio workstation (show screenshot)
      - We'll learn later why the amplitude spectrum doesn't look as clean as you might expect
  - Intuition: what is the Fourier Transform doing?
    - The Fourier Transform might seem quite mysterious at this point. That's okay!
    - How is the Fourier Transform able to pick out the amplitude of a particular sinusoid?
    - Let's unpack it a bit more intuitively, inspired by this [3blue1brown video](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=spUNpyF58BY).
    - What does the Fourier transform do to determine how much of frequency \omega is in x(t)?
      - Synthesizes a complex sinusoid at -\omega (just a change of direction)
      - Multiplies this complex sinusoid by the sound to measure the similarity between that sound and periodic behavior at \omega
      - Integrates from -\inf to \in to sum this similarity over time
    - Intuitively, we're measuring the correlation between x(t) and an analytical device operating at a frequency \omega of interest
